  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "Q3O+Y8OKCNbmdWprc5UtbcSWn+U=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:19.783Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The `external-feed-ingest` recipe states its thesis as one line: **the document is the checkpoint store.** Both the dedup set and the high-watermark live in document state, and the worker rebuilds them from state at startup via `seedFromState()`. An in-memory-only checkpoint is the bug the recipe exists to prevent \u2014 crash the worker mid-stream and it re-ingests everything since the last flush.\n\nThe subtler half is *which* value is the dedup key. It is `(source, externalId)`, **not** the watermark:\n\n> The watermark is only a fetch optimization (\"don't re-read the whole feed\"). It is not the dedup mechanism, because real feeds redeliver: the same `externalId` can reappear at a *later* cursor, sailing straight past a watermark filter.\n\nSo the two do different jobs. The watermark tracks the feed's monotonic *delivery cursor* and bounds how much you re-read; the id-set answers the authoritative question \"have I already recorded this?\" On restart the id-set is what saves you.\n\nThe state schema keeps both in the document:\n\n```graphql\ntype FeedLedgerState {\n  source: String!\n  watermark: Int!\n  entries: [LedgerEntry!]!\n}\n```\n\nWatermark advancement is folded into the domain operations (`max(watermark, sequence)`, so an out-of-order delivery cannot regress it) rather than living in a separate `setWatermark` op. And the reducer enforces uniqueness too, throwing `DuplicateEntry` \u2014 defence in depth against racing redeliveries that both pass the worker's in-memory check.\n\nCorrections are modelled as explicit operations, never mutations: `markSuperseded` flips the old entry's status, sets `supersededBy`, and appends the corrected value as a *new* `RECORDED` entry. The original payload is never touched.\n\nThe same pattern appears in `inbound-webhook-bridge` for the push direction, where provider `event.id` values accumulate in the document's `processedEventIds`. Notably the recipe argues the crash-safety property is independent of where the loop lives \u2014 a long-running script or an `IProcessor` both work, because the guarantee comes from seeding state from the document.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`, External Feed Ingest recipe (\"The one idea worth taking away\", \"Why externalId is the dedup key\"), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:39:18.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:19.783Z"
    }
  },
